Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit


Adds ps4-led.c and ps4-led.h, a Linux LED class driver for the PS4
front panel LED on Aeolia (CXD90025G) and Belize southbridges.

Based on the LED control script from PsxItArch Linux. Thanks to Saya
for documenting the supported colors and payload format, preventing
us from sending garbage to the EMC and frying the LEDs.

The ICC LED payload format (major 0x09, minor 0x20, 35 bytes) has been
fully decoded via cross-reference of the Belize ps4-linux led_config
source against Aeolia reverse-engineered payloads. Channel IDs,
operation structure, and intensity byte offsets are identical across
both hardware revisions. One driver covers all PS4 models.

Exposes eleven LED class nodes under /sys/class/leds/:
  ps4:blue, ps4:white, ps4:orange, ps4:orange_blue, ps4:orange_white,
  ps4:pulsate_orange, ps4:orange_white_blue, ps4:white_blue,
  ps4:violet_blue, ps4:pink, ps4:pink_blue (all :status suffix)

Also exposes two platform device sysfs attributes:
  mode              - static (default) or thermal
  thermal_interval_ms - poll interval in ms (default 2000)

In thermal mode a delayed_work polls APU temperature via ICC 0x0B/0x01
and drives the LED automatically:
  Blue   = APU temp < 65°C
  White  = 65°C to 79°C
  Orange = >= 80°C

The worker is never scheduled until user-space explicitly enables
thermal mode. cancel_delayed_work_sync() in remove() ensures the
worker has fully exited before the device is freed, preventing
use-after-free. The mutex is always released before calling
cancel_delayed_work_sync() to avoid deadlock with the work function.

Signed-off-by: rmux <armandas.kvietkus@proton.me>
